          "text": "filename: docs/knife-sharpening.md\n---\n# How to Sharpen a Kitchen Knife by Hand\n\n## Gather Your Tools  \nBefore you begin, gather the necessary tools to ensure a safe and effective sharpening process. You will need a high-quality whetstone—preferably a natural or synthetic stone with a medium to fine grit, such as 800 or 1000 grit. A honing rod is optional but helpful for maintaining edge alignment. A clean, dry towel is essential for wiping the knife after sharpening. Additionally, have a cutting board and a small bowl of water nearby to keep the whetstone moist during use. A knife block or a protective container will help store the knife safely after sharpening. Make sure your work surface is stable and free of clutter to avoid accidents. A magnifying glass or a small flashlight can assist in inspecting the edge for imperfections. Always wear gloves if you have sensitive skin or are working with a particularly sharp edge.\n\n## Sharpen the Edge  \nHold the knife at a consistent angle—typically between 15 and 20 degrees—relative to the whetstone. This angle is crucial for creating a sharp, durable edge. Place the beveled edge of the knife on the stone and push it forward in a smooth, even motion, applying light pressure. Move the knife in a straight line from the heel to the tip, then repeat the motion on the opposite side. For best results, use a back-and-forth motion, ensuring the entire edge is evenly sharpened. Keep the stone wet with water throughout the process to prevent overheating and to maintain a smooth cutting surface. After each pass, wipe the knife with a towel to remove debris and excess water. Repeat this process 5 to 10 times on each side until the knife feels significantly sharper. Always maintain a consistent angle to avoid creating a dull or uneven edge.\n\n## Test and Store  \nAfter sharpening, test the knife on a piece of paper or a soft food item like a carrot. A properly sharpened knife should glide smoothly without requiring excessive force. If the knife still feels dull or struggles to cut, repeat the sharpening process with a slightly finer grit whetstone. Once satisfied with the edge, clean the knife thoroughly with warm, soapy water and dry it completely. Store the knife in a knife block, a magnetic strip, or a sheath to protect the edge from damage. Avoid stacking knives directly on top of each other, as this can dull the edge over time. Regular maintenance using a whetstone and proper angle will extend the life of your kitchen knife and keep it ready for daily use.",
